  "live_records": {
    "q-48741": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48741",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}?$",
      "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+4)-x\\cdot ({x}^{2}+4)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
