    "q-48789": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48789",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{3}-3x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-3x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-3x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-3x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-3){e}^{{x}^{3}-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-3){e}^{{x}^{3}-3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-3x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-3x}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at  $x=1$ and $x=-1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
